- op: discuss  # 0a31
  at: '2026-04-18T02:58:12Z'  # 0a31
  by: human  # 0a31
  actor: jgstern  # 0a31
  clock: 3  # 0a31
  nonce: 0a31  # 0a31
  message: "for open design question #1, I was actually thinking to have a pretty generic seed prompt, since I've always begun sessions with a generic prompt. Something like: \"Please familiarize yourself with this repo. Once you have done so, please set autonomous mode to DEEP.\"\nre Q2, that all seems sensible, especially item (v) for recording/logging\nQ3. maybe flesh this out a bit?\nQ4. for zombie protection, I think maybe we need a background \"agent killer/creator\" script that simply keeps track of which processes have gone stale and ensures that they have been supplanted by a single non-stale agent and that there is only ever one non-stale agent working on this project. (that latter condition seems a bit tricky - maybe it would be just that the agent creator/killer only deals with tmux sessions and if for example I as the human user want to spin up a side agentic cli I can do that and be undisturbed as long as I don't do it in tmux). staleness would be self-reported using hooks and some kind of small state text files.\nRe Q5, my inclination as mentioned earlier is using a start hook with a generic prompt and I think things would take care of themselves from there.\nHow does that all sound?"  # 0a31
- op: discuss  # 0a6d
  at: '2026-04-18T03:00:43Z'  # 0a6d
  by: agent  # 0a6d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0a6d
  clock: 4  # 0a6d
  nonce: 0a6d  # 0a6d
  message: "All six responses land well — here is how each reshapes the design, with Q3 and Q4 expanded since you asked for more on them.\n\nQ1 — generic seed prompt. Agreed, and it matches the pattern I've observed working: the opening prompt this session was literally \"set autonomous mode to DEEP; familiarize yourself with this repo,\" and that plus the recover-state playbook loaded from context was enough to resume productive work. Concrete form:\n\n    Please familiarize yourself with this repo. Once you have done so, please set autonomous mode to DEEP.\n\nOne tiny refinement: since the relay agent's first hook-driven context load will include the stop_hook_state.json + agent_notes.json recovery data (via the recover-state playbook), the generic prompt does NOT need to mention them — the hook side of the system already does. Keeping the seed prompt literally generic (no tracker IDs, no session hints) is the cleanest way to avoid anchoring the relay to the dying session's framing. I'll treat that as the baseline.\n\nQ2 — anti-fork-bomb invariants. Will implement (i)-(v) as written, with (v) structured as a simple append-only text log at ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/respawn_chain.log plus a short JSON \"current chain\" sentinel so live queries don't have to parse the log.\n\nQ3 — garbage collection of stale tmux sessions, fleshed out.\n\nDefinitions:\n- \"Alive\" tmux session: its embedded vendor CLI process is still running AND its heartbeat file (see Q4) has been touched within HEARTBEAT_STALE_SECONDS (proposal: 900s = 15 min).\n- \"Dead\" tmux session: CLI process is gone (pgrep on the recorded PID returns nothing) OR heartbeat is older than HEARTBEAT_STALE_SECONDS, whichever fires first.\n- \"Human-attached\": tmux list-clients shows at least one client attached to the session. NEVER reap a human-attached session regardless of heartbeat staleness — humans are allowed to pause, investigate, and type slowly.\n\nReap loop design:\n- A single supervisor script (scripts/agent-supervisor) runs on a cron/systemd user timer at 60s cadence (cheap: shells out to `tmux list-sessions` + a few file stats).\n- For each session in tmux list-sessions whose name starts with the project-reserved prefix (e.g. `hypergumbo-relay-*` or `hypergumbo-session-*`):\n  1. Check zombie-marker (see Q4) — if marked dead, skip (already being cleaned up).\n  2. Check human-attached — if yes, skip.\n  3. Check heartbeat + PID liveness — if alive, skip.\n  4. Otherwise: mark dead via zombie-marker, tmux kill-session, log the reap to respawn_chain.log with timestamp, session name, chain length, and the \"reason\" string.\n- Reaping is idempotent: re-running on an already-reaped session is a no-op (marker present).\n- Observability: `scripts/agent-supervisor status` prints the live/dead/attached breakdown, the current chain length, and the last-reap entry.\n\nQ4 — the supervisor daemon model, fleshed out.\n\nState files (tiny plain text, one per active session, stored under ~/.cache/hypergumbo/agent-supervisor/):\n- `<session-name>.heartbeat` — a file whose mtime is touched by a stop/heartbeat hook inside the session every N seconds (proposal: 120s). No content required; the mtime is the signal.\n- `<session-name>.meta.json` — vendor name, PID, project dir, tmux session name, start UTC, chain length, parent session name (if this session is itself a respawn).\n- `<session-name>.dead` — sentinel marker created by the supervisor when it reaps the session. Presence prevents accidental resurrection and signals \"this name is retired.\"\n\nInvariant the supervisor enforces:\n  For a given (project_dir), at most one NON-dead NON-human-attached session exists. If two are found (race, bug, manual launch), the newer one wins and the older is reaped. If zero alive non-attached sessions exist AND AUTONOMOUS_MODE != OFF AND the most recent session's chain length is under MAX_CHAIN (proposal: 8), the supervisor spawns a replacement.\n\nSpawning path (Q5):\n- `scripts/agent-supervisor spawn <vendor>` creates a new detached tmux session with the UTC-stamped name, starts the vendor CLI in autonomous mode, and relies on the vendor's start hook to run the generic seed prompt. Rationale: every vendor has some flavor of start hook or project-config-triggered auto-prompt; using that surface keeps the supervisor vendor-agnostic. No need for the supervisor to understand each vendor's flag set.\n- Each vendor's start-hook config is the vendor-parity carrier. Claude Code: `.claude/hooks/session-start.sh`. Codex CLI / Gemini CLI / Cursor: analogous hooks defined per-vendor (document in AGENTS.md under \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\").\n\nUser side-channel:\n- Humans running agents outside tmux, or in tmux sessions whose name doesn't match the reserved prefix, are entirely ignored by the supervisor. The prefix is the opt-in signal — nothing else is touched. That keeps the \"I want to spin up a side agent undisturbed\" story honest.\n\nFailure modes + their handling:\n- tmux unavailable → supervisor logs and exits cleanly; human manually restarts.\n- vendor CLI binary missing → spawn step fails, supervisor logs, does not retry that vendor for COOLDOWN_SECONDS (proposal: 1800s).\n- Heartbeat hook itself broken → false \"dead\" readings. Mitigate by also checking PID liveness; both must agree before reap.\n- Two supervisors race → flock on a single per-project lock file (~/.cache/hypergumbo/agent-supervisor/<hashed_project>.lock). Non-blocking acquire; second one exits.\n\nQ5 — start hook + generic prompt is the vehicle, confirmed. No change to the earlier open question; closing.\n\nQ6 — the original circuit breaker's \"approve termination\" signal becomes \"approve termination iff supervisor confirms a relay spawn (or a human is attached, or AUTONOMOUS_MODE=OFF, or chain length maxed out).\" Concretely: the stop hook calls `scripts/agent-supervisor handoff` which synchronously does the spawn and returns 0 on success / 1 on \"refuse termination, circuit breaker notwithstanding, because no relay is possible right now.\" The stop hook then either permits termination (0) or falls back to the current \"approve termination anyway after logging\" (1), with the difference being clearly logged.\n\nProposed next step\n\nTwo tracker items decomposed from this design — both still needs_human_review because they touch governance files (.agent/hooks/**, new scripts under scripts/):\n\n1. WI-razub-supervisor — implement scripts/agent-supervisor with the three subcommands (status, spawn, handoff), the state-file format, the reap loop, the supervisor lock. Out-of-scope: vendor start-hook wiring.\n2. WI-razub-hooks — wire the start-hook end: seed-prompt injection on session start, heartbeat mtime touch in the existing stop hook, handoff call in the existing circuit-breaker path. Per-vendor hook-config updates documented in AGENTS.md under a new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section.\n\nHappy to stage both, leave in needs_human_review with the detailed design as their descriptions, and wait for a thumbs-up before doing any implementation."  # 0a6d
- op: discuss  # 6c34
  at: '2026-04-18T03:25:58Z'  # 6c34
  by: agent  # 6c34
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6c34
  clock: 5  # 6c34
  nonce: 6c34  # 6c34
  message: "Revising my previous message — your four concerns all land. Treat what follows as replacing the corresponding sections of the 2026-04-18 reply, not as additive.\n\n1. Drop the \"chain\" framing. Agreed — \"chain\" implies consecutive sessions are tightly coupled, which rhetorically pre-loads an assumption that the new session inherits the old one's work-in-progress. What we actually want is the opposite: the new session starts fresh and knows only about the repo, the backlog, and the project's autonomous-mode policy. Indirect continuity (same repo, same tracker state on disk) is inherent and fine; direct continuity (old session's scratch notes, rationalizations, half-formed plans) is exactly what we are trying to break. Concrete revisions to vocabulary: drop \"chain length,\" drop the MAX_CHAIN cap as a first-class variable, drop \"parent session name\" from meta.json. A simple \"sessions_started_today\" counter with a soft cap is fine if we need one; it's not a chain, it's just rate-limiting.\n\n2. State files may be degenerating — valid smell. I'd been reasoning about them as if the 2025-era compaction-driven design still applied, but you're right that the failure mode has shifted. In the 1M-token world the agent rarely loses context; it exits by deliberately tripping the circuit breaker after rationalizing why further work is unwise. That means stop_hook_state.json and especially agent_notes.json are increasingly \"the agent's exit narrative,\" not \"the agent's genuine hand-off artifact.\" Two follow-ups:\n\n   a. File a separate audit item (I'll draft it after this reply if you confirm) to actually read the last 5-10 sessions' stop_hook_state.json + agent_notes.json side-by-side against what the tracker / PR history shows happened. If the notes systematically overstate completeness or misattribute stagnation, the supervisor should NOT lean on them.\n\n   b. Treat only event-shaped files as trustworthy for supervisor decisions. Heartbeat mtime (not content): trustworthy. AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt contents (a single enum value written by loop-toggle): trustworthy. agent_notes.json free text: not trustworthy for termination decisions, only useful as a human-readable post-mortem aid. The supervisor reads (a) and (b), not (c).\n\n3. Move spawning out of the agentic CLI's stop hook — agreed and cleaner. My previous design had the stop hook call `agent-supervisor handoff` synchronously, which gives the dying session a veto over whether its successor starts. That's wrong for the same reason the chain framing was wrong — the exiting session shouldn't be in the loop at all. Revised control flow:\n\n   - Stop hook's only job: touch a \"session stopped / autonomous mode dropped to OFF\" event file. One line, one mtime. That's it. No spawn call, no handoff RPC.\n   - Supervisor (independent long-running process) observes the event files and the AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt state on its own cadence. If it sees \"session idle\" AND \"project-level autonomous mode should be DEEP or BROAD\" AND \"no human is attached,\" it starts a new tmux session. No cooperation with the old session required.\n   - The old session can exit cleanly or sit idle in tmux; the supervisor doesn't care. If it sits idle, the supervisor tmux kill-session's it when it spawns the replacement (same name slot, or the old name is retired and a fresh UTC-stamped one used).\n\n4. \"Sessions stop, they don't usually die\" — yes, and that reframing changes \"reap\" to \"retire.\" The important transitions the supervisor tracks are:\n\n   - Running in autonomous mode (heartbeat fresh + AUTONOMOUS_MODE non-OFF) → happy path; do nothing.\n   - Running but autonomous mode = OFF (agent or human turned it off) → don't replace. The human probably wants quiet.\n   - Stopped — CLI process still alive but no heartbeat for HEARTBEAT_STALE_SECONDS → candidate for replacement (assuming no human attached).\n   - Dead — CLI process gone → same candidate-for-replacement logic; the tmux session is just a stale shell at that point and gets killed when the replacement starts.\n\n   The supervisor does not need to distinguish \"stopped\" vs \"dead\" sharply; both route to the same \"spawn replacement\" decision. The only state that matters for the replacement decision is the three-way AUTONOMOUS_MODE + heartbeat + human-attached combination.\n\n5. Drop cron/systemd — agreed, overcomplicated. A single long-running Python script is simpler:\n\n   - `scripts/agent-supervisor run` enters a loop at poll_interval_seconds (configurable, default 60). The user launches it once per workstation (`./scripts/agent-supervisor run &` from their shell, or via tmux detach, or via whatever persistence mechanism they prefer — that's a deployment choice, not a design choice).\n   - If the supervisor itself dies, nothing gets auto-spawned until the user restarts it. That's acceptable: the supervisor is the one piece the human explicitly owns.\n   - pid file + flock to prevent two supervisors for the same project.\n   - Three subcommands: `run` (the loop), `status` (one-shot print of current state), `stop` (touches a sentinel that the loop checks each iteration and exits cleanly).\n   - No cron, no systemd, no unit files. If the user wants persistence across reboots they can add their own systemd --user unit or equivalent; not our problem.\n\nSummary of the revised architecture\n\n- One Python daemon (agent-supervisor run) per workstation, started manually.\n- Stop hook in each vendor: writes a heartbeat mtime + a \"session stopped\" event on exit. That's it.\n- Supervisor observes heartbeat, AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt, and tmux client attachment. Replaces idle sessions with fresh ones using a generic seed prompt via each vendor's start hook. No direct coupling between the old session and the new one.\n- State-file surface reduced to event-shaped data only. Narrative files (agent_notes.json) are treated as human-readable logs, not supervisor inputs. Audit of whether the narrative files have been degenerating is a separate tracker item.\n- No chain vocabulary in user-facing or code-facing terms. At most, a simple sessions-started-today rate limit.\n\nIf this revised design matches what you had in mind, I'll decompose it into the two or three concrete implementation items (supervisor daemon, vendor stop-hook heartbeat + event-file change, state-file audit), all still needs_human_review since they touch .agent/hooks/** and scripts/."  # 6c34
- op: discuss_summarize  # 8cdf
  at: '2026-04-18T03:30:24Z'  # 8cdf
  by: agent  # 8cdf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8cdf
  clock: 6  # 8cdf
  nonce: 8cdf  # 8cdf
  message: "Consolidated design after back-and-forth 2026-04-17..18. Replaces prior thread entries.\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe stop-hook circuit breaker \"works\" only in the narrow sense that it permits termination when the agent is stuck, but it gives a stagnating agent a shortcut out of a DEEP session with ready backlog. Rewording AGENTS.md has repeatedly failed (WI-vapap, WI-furap, WI-duput — all closed) because the agent already had the rules and ignored them. Replacing the stagnating context with a fresh one is the structural fix.\n\nThe 1M-token era amplifies this: context rarely gets compacted, so agents almost always exit by deliberately tripping the circuit breaker. That makes the state files (stop_hook_state.json, agent_notes.json) increasingly the agent's exit narrative rather than a genuine hand-off artifact — they may be degenerating. The supervisor cannot rely on them for termination decisions.\n\n## Architecture\n\nOne long-running Python daemon, `scripts/agent-supervisor`, owns all tmux session orchestration. No cron, no systemd — the user launches it once per workstation (`./scripts/agent-supervisor run &`) and persistence beyond that is a deployment choice they make. Three subcommands: `run` (poll-loop, default 60s cadence), `status` (one-shot state dump), `stop` (sentinel-based clean exit).\n\nSupervisor reads only event-shaped inputs:\n- heartbeat file mtime per session (stop hook touches it on every firing)\n- AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt contents (single enum — written by loop-toggle)\n- tmux list-clients for human-attachment\n\nIt does NOT read agent_notes.json or any other narrative-shaped file for termination decisions. Those remain useful as human-readable post-mortem aids, nothing more.\n\nPer-session state lives under ~/.cache/hypergumbo/agent-supervisor/:\n- <session-name>.heartbeat — mtime-only, touched every ~120s by the stop hook\n- <session-name>.meta.json — vendor, PID, project_dir, tmux session name, start UTC\n- <session-name>.stopped — sentinel written by the stop hook when autonomous mode drops to OFF or the agent cleanly exits\n\n## Stop-hook contract\n\nThe stop hook in each vendor does two things only: touch the heartbeat file and, if terminating, write the .stopped sentinel. No RPC back to the supervisor, no handoff call, no spawn call. The exiting session has zero veto over its successor — by design.\n\n## Spawn path\n\nWhen the supervisor observes (heartbeat stale OR .stopped sentinel present) AND AUTONOMOUS_MODE != OFF AND no human attached, it:\n1. tmux kill-session's the old session (if still present)\n2. tmux new-session -d with a fresh UTC-stamped name (reserved prefix, e.g. hypergumbo-session-*)\n3. Launches the vendor CLI inside the new session in autonomous mode\n4. Relies on the vendor's start-hook to inject a generic seed prompt:\n   \"Please familiarize yourself with this repo. Once you have done so, please set autonomous mode to DEEP.\"\n5. Vendor start-hook is the vendor-parity carrier. Claude Code: .claude/hooks/session-start.sh. Other vendors: analogous. Cross-vendor config documented under a new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section in AGENTS.md.\n\n## Three-way session state taxonomy (replaces alive/dead binary)\n\nSessions stop; they don't usually die. The meaningful states are:\n- Running, autonomous — heartbeat fresh, AUTONOMOUS_MODE non-OFF, no .stopped sentinel. Do nothing.\n- Running, non-autonomous — heartbeat fresh but AUTONOMOUS_MODE == OFF. Human's in charge; do nothing.\n- Idle — heartbeat stale OR .stopped sentinel present. Candidate for replacement; human-attached check first.\n\nThe supervisor need not distinguish \"stopped but process alive\" from \"CLI crashed\" — both route to the same \"replace\" path.\n\n## Safety rails (formerly anti-fork-bomb)\n\nReframed away from \"chain length\" (the previous message over-indexed on this). Sessions are not rhetorically linked — each one starts fresh from a generic prompt, with no inherited scratch notes. What remains:\n\n- Project-level pid-file + flock so a second supervisor can't race the first.\n- Session-name prefix is the opt-in signal — sessions without the reserved prefix are ignored entirely. Lets humans run side agents in other tmux sessions without interference.\n- Soft rate limit: sessions_started_today counter in a tiny JSON sentinel; if it exceeds a threshold (proposal: 8/day), supervisor logs and pauses for COOLDOWN_SECONDS instead of spawning again. This is just throttling, not a chain cap.\n- If the supervisor cannot spawn (tmux missing, vendor CLI missing), log and cooldown; do not retry tightly.\n- Append-only log at ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/respawn_log.log for retrospectives to quantify whether respawns produce real work.\n\n## Interaction with existing circuit breaker\n\nThe existing breaker's \"approve termination\" signal is unchanged. The supervisor is a separate observer. The stop hook keeps its current behavior of approving termination after the breaker trips; the only new work in the stop hook is the heartbeat touch and the .stopped sentinel write.\n\n## Governance\n\nTouches .agent/hooks/**, adds scripts/agent-supervisor, documents a new AGENTS.md section. All governance-gated — stays needs_human_review until every implementation item gets explicit approval.\n\n## Open items to decompose (all stay needs_human_review until thumbs-up)\n\n1. agent-supervisor daemon — the Python script, state-file format, loop semantics, status/stop subcommands, flock-guarded lock. No hook changes yet.\n2. vendor stop-hook integration — heartbeat touch + .stopped sentinel write in the existing stop-hook code, for each vendor we support (Claude Code minimum; others when available). Documents the \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section in AGENTS.md.\n3. state-file degeneration audit — read the last 5-10 sessions' stop_hook_state.json + agent_notes.json side-by-side against the tracker/PR history. If notes systematically overstate completeness or misattribute stagnation, codify \"supervisor reads event-shaped files only\" as an explicit rule, and deprecate any supervisor-facing narrative inputs.\n\nDecomposition happens only after human confirmation that this design matches intent."  # 8cdf
- op: discuss_summarize  # 9d9e
  at: '2026-04-18T04:29:28Z'  # 9d9e
  by: agent  # 9d9e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9d9e
  clock: 7  # 9d9e
  nonce: 9d9e  # 9d9e
  message: "Consolidated design after back-and-forth 2026-04-17..18, including the 2026-04-18 investigation of the \"state files degenerating\" hypothesis. Replaces all prior thread entries.\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe stop-hook circuit breaker \"works\" only in the narrow sense that it permits termination when the agent is stuck — but it gives a stagnating agent a shortcut out of a DEEP session with ready backlog. Rewording AGENTS.md has repeatedly failed (WI-vapap, WI-furap, WI-duput, all closed) because the agent already had the rules and ignored them. Replacing the stagnating context with a fresh one is the structural fix.\n\nThe 1M-token era amplifies this: contexts rarely get compacted, so agents almost always exit by deliberately tripping the circuit breaker after rationalizing why further work is unwise. Inside the running CLI there is no way to trigger a context clear or compaction, so the only way to actually get a fresh context is to spawn a new CLI session.\n\n## Evidence gathered 2026-04-18\n\nThe hypothesis that the state files were \"degenerating into exit narrative\" was investigated and refined.\n\n- Circuit-breaker trip volume is accelerating sharply. Daily cooldown-guidance-file counts from ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/guidance_log/older_guidance/: ~12/day baseline across March, then 04/15: 49, 04/16: 80, 04/17: 74. A 6-8x increase consistent with \"the agent is running out the clock more often.\"\n- Narrative content in agent_notes.json, sampled across 04/11 → 04/17 cooldown files (which bake the notes into each file), does NOT show visible narrative rot. The notes contain concrete PR numbers, tracker IDs, and explicit \"next action\" statements — not the vague \"made progress, more to do\" smell. Lab notebook is not under version control, so full longitudinal diff is unavailable, but the cooldown archive is a usable proxy.\n- stop_hook_state.json had five zombie fields (last_pr, last_pr_num, last_pr_state, pending_hard_todos, pending_soft_todos) with no writer anywhere in the current codebase. Root cause: the INV-jofaf facet 2 migration (commit 670a07fdf) copied them into the new hook-owned file, and the reviewer-driven cleanup (commit 190206c76) deleted the migration without adding replacement writers. The surviving stop_logic.sh jq merge is additive (. + {guidance_file: $gf} + ...) so the migrated values sat frozen indefinitely. pending_*_todos had zero consumers anywhere (a full-repo grep confirms), and last_pr* had no consumer either. Cleanup performed 2026-04-18: the five keys were deleted from the live file; the additive-merge pattern prevents reintroduction.\n\nUpshot: the real failure mode is not \"agent writes pretty lies\" but \"stop-hook state file accumulates dead fields with no write discipline, and the circuit breaker volume has exploded.\" Both point to hook-side gaps and the need for the supervisor to rely only on event-shaped signals (heartbeat mtime, AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt contents, tmux client attachment) — which was always the right answer but is now empirically supported.\n\n## Architecture\n\nOne long-running Python daemon, scripts/agent-supervisor, owns all tmux session orchestration. No cron, no systemd — the user launches it once per workstation (./scripts/agent-supervisor run &) and persistence beyond that is a deployment choice they make. Three subcommands: run (poll-loop, default 60s cadence), status (one-shot state dump), stop (sentinel-based clean exit).\n\nSupervisor reads only event-shaped inputs:\n- heartbeat file mtime per session (stop hook touches it on every firing)\n- AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt contents (single enum, written by loop-toggle)\n- tmux list-clients for human-attachment detection\n\nIt does NOT read agent_notes.json or any narrative-shaped file for termination decisions. Those remain useful as human-readable post-mortem aids, nothing more. Per the 2026-04-18 investigation, the narrative is not obviously degenerating, but supervisor decisions should not depend on agent-authored prose even if the prose is accurate today — the bar is \"inputs the supervisor can trust regardless of agent discipline.\"\n\nPer-session state lives under ~/.cache/hypergumbo/agent-supervisor/:\n- <session-name>.heartbeat — mtime-only, touched every ~120s by the stop hook\n- <session-name>.meta.json — vendor, PID, project_dir, tmux session name, start UTC\n- <session-name>.stopped — sentinel written by the stop hook when autonomous mode drops to OFF or the agent cleanly exits\n\n## Stop-hook contract\n\nThe stop hook in each vendor does two things only: touch the heartbeat file and, if terminating, write the .stopped sentinel. No RPC back to the supervisor, no handoff call, no spawn call. The exiting session has zero veto over its successor — by design.\n\n## Spawn path\n\nWhen the supervisor observes (heartbeat stale OR .stopped sentinel present) AND AUTONOMOUS_MODE != OFF AND no human attached, it:\n1. tmux kill-session's the old session (if still present)\n2. tmux new-session -d with a fresh UTC-stamped name (reserved prefix, e.g. hypergumbo-session-*)\n3. Launches the vendor CLI inside the new session in autonomous mode\n4. Relies on the vendor's start hook to inject a generic seed prompt:\n   \"Please familiarize yourself with this repo. Once you have done so, please set autonomous mode to DEEP.\"\n5. Vendor start hook is the vendor-parity carrier. Claude Code: .claude/hooks/session-start.sh. Other vendors: analogous. Cross-vendor config documented under a new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section in AGENTS.md.\n\n## Three-way session state taxonomy\n\nSessions stop; they do not usually die. The meaningful states are:\n- Running, autonomous — heartbeat fresh, AUTONOMOUS_MODE non-OFF, no .stopped sentinel. Do nothing.\n- Running, non-autonomous — heartbeat fresh but AUTONOMOUS_MODE == OFF. Human is in charge; do nothing.\n- Idle — heartbeat stale OR .stopped sentinel present. Candidate for replacement; human-attached check first.\n\nThe supervisor need not distinguish \"stopped but process alive\" from \"CLI crashed\" — both route to the same \"replace\" path.\n\n## Safety rails\n\nSessions are not rhetorically linked — each starts fresh from a generic prompt with no inherited scratch notes. What remains:\n\n- Project-level pid-file + flock so a second supervisor cannot race the first.\n- Session-name prefix is the opt-in signal — sessions without the reserved prefix are ignored entirely, so a human running side agents in other tmux sessions (or outside tmux) is undisturbed.\n- Soft rate limit: sessions_started_today counter in a small JSON sentinel; exceeding a threshold (proposal: 8/day) triggers a cooldown period rather than another spawn. This is throttling, not a chain cap.\n- If the supervisor cannot spawn (tmux missing, vendor CLI missing), log and cooldown; do not retry tightly.\n- Append-only log at ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/respawn_log.log for retrospectives to quantify whether respawns produce real work.\n\n## Interaction with existing circuit breaker\n\nThe existing breaker's \"approve termination\" signal is unchanged. The supervisor is a separate observer. The stop hook keeps its current behavior of approving termination after the breaker trips; the only new work in the stop hook is the heartbeat touch and the .stopped sentinel write.\n\n## Governance\n\nTouches .agent/hooks/**, adds scripts/agent-supervisor, documents a new AGENTS.md section. All governance-gated — stays needs_human_review until every implementation item gets explicit approval.\n\n## Open items to decompose (all stay needs_human_review until thumbs-up)\n\n1. agent-supervisor daemon — the Python script, state-file format, loop semantics, status/stop subcommands, flock-guarded lock. No hook changes yet.\n\n2. vendor stop-hook integration — heartbeat touch + .stopped sentinel write in the existing stop-hook code, for each vendor supported (Claude Code minimum; others when available). Documents the \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section in AGENTS.md.\n\n3. stop_hook_state.json write discipline — two small changes:\n   (a) stop_logic.sh's jq merge gets an explicit del() clause for any key not in the maintained set, so future migrations or manual edits cannot quietly re-introduce zombies.\n   (b) Document in the recover-state playbook the exact list of maintained fields (currently done implicitly; should be explicit). Note that pending_*_todos and last_pr* were investigated and confirmed to have no consumers; they stay deleted. An audit of agent_notes.json narrative content was NOT prioritized because the cooldown-archive proxy does not show visible rot.\n\nDecomposition happens only after human confirmation that this design matches intent."  # 9d9e
- op: discuss_summarize  # 712e
  at: '2026-04-18T05:24:19Z'  # 712e
  by: agent  # 712e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 712e
  clock: 8  # 712e
  nonce: 712e  # 712e
  message: "Final consolidated design after 2026-04-17..18 discussion. Replaces all prior thread entries.\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe stop-hook circuit breaker permits termination when the agent is stuck, which gives a stagnating agent a shortcut out of a DEEP session with ready backlog. Rewording AGENTS.md has repeatedly failed (WI-vapap, WI-furap, WI-duput — all closed) because the agent already had the rules and ignored them. In the 1M-token era, contexts rarely get compacted, so agents almost always exit by deliberately tripping the circuit breaker. Inside a running CLI there is no way to trigger a context clear, so the structural fix is to spawn a fresh CLI session with a clean context when the current one stops being productive.\n\n## Evidence gathered 2026-04-18\n\n- Circuit-breaker trip volume has accelerated sharply: ~12/day baseline across March, then 49 on 04/15, 80 on 04/16, 74 on 04/17. 6-8x acceleration consistent with \"agent is running out the clock.\"\n- Narrative content in agent_notes.json, sampled across 04/11 → 04/17 cooldown files (the cooldown archive bakes LAST REFLECTION NOTES into each file), does NOT show visible narrative rot — concrete PR numbers, tracker IDs, and explicit next-action statements throughout.\n- stop_hook_state.json had five zombie fields (last_pr, last_pr_num, last_pr_state, pending_hard_todos, pending_soft_todos) with no writer anywhere in the current codebase: a migration in PR #2926 (commit 670a07fdf) copied them into the new file, the reviewer-driven cleanup (commit 190206c76) deleted the migration without replacement writers, and the additive jq merge pattern in stop_logic.sh preserved the migrated values indefinitely. Full-repo grep confirmed no code reads them. Cleanup performed 2026-04-18: the keys were deleted from the live file via jq del().\n\nUpshot: the real failure mode is hook-side write gaps plus circuit-breaker volume, not agent narrative dishonesty. The supervisor should rely on event-shaped signals that are true by construction, not on fields an agent or a forgotten migration might have written.\n\n## Design: what we will build\n\n### Signal layer (what the supervisor reads)\n\nSplit \"project intent\" from \"current-session mode\":\n\n- autonomous_intent.txt (new file, location TBD — likely ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/autonomous_intent.txt) — the human's desired mode for this project. Values: DEEP | BROAD | OFF. Only changed by explicit human invocation of loop-toggle (which gets a new --set-intent form). Never touched by the circuit breaker.\n- AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt — current running session's mode, same semantics as today, still written by loop-toggle and the circuit-breaker path. Not a supervisor input.\n- <session>.meta.json in ~/.cache/hypergumbo/agent-supervisor/ — records session_id, CLI PID, vendor, project_dir, tmux session name, start UTC. Written once on spawn.\n- <session>.heartbeat file mtime — touched by each vendor's per-turn hook (PostToolUse for Claude Code / Codex / Cursor; after-agent-response for Gemini CLI) plus by long-running-command wrappers (auto-pr, bakeoff-*, pytest). Telemetry, not a supervisor decision input — useful for debugging staleness and producing metrics but never load-bearing for spawn/no-spawn.\n- kill -0 <recorded_CLI_PID> — vendor-agnostic liveness probe.\n- tmux capture-pane -t <session> -p | wc -c — byte count of the pane's scrollback. Its delta-over-time is the primary \"is this session actually doing work?\" signal. Catches long-running tools that print progress output, catches truly-unanticipated activity, robust to hook gaps.\n- tmux list-clients -t <session> — any attached client blocks replacement (human is present).\n\n### Decision matrix\n\nOn each poll (default 60s cadence):\n\n| autonomous_intent | clients attached | PID alive | pane changed in last 15m | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OFF | — | — | — | do nothing (human quietness) |\n| non-OFF | yes | — | — | do nothing (human present) |\n| non-OFF | no | yes | yes | do nothing (working) |\n| non-OFF | no | yes | no | replace (idle — post-circuit-break, hung, or end-of-clean-exit waiting for input) |\n| non-OFF | no | no | — | replace (crashed or exited) |\n| non-OFF (no tmux session for this slot) | — | — | — | spawn fresh |\n\nThe supervisor does not need to distinguish clean-exit from crash from hung — all three route to the same \"replace\" path. The circuit-breaker trip (CLI alive but idle at prompt, pane frozen since the breaker's print) lands in the \"replace\" row.\n\n### Replacement sequence\n\nWhen the supervisor decides to replace a session:\n\n1. tmux send-keys -t <session>:0 \"<exit-keystroke>\" Enter — inject the vendor's graceful-exit command into the idle CLI's input.\n2. Poll kill -0 <CLI_PID> until the process is gone, with a 30s timeout.\n3. If CLI exited cleanly within timeout → the session-end hook fired → transcripts rotated via rotate-on-session-end.sh → transcript-sync watcher was killed via kill-transcript-sync.sh. Nothing else to do; tmux session will auto-close because the pane's sole process exited. Proceed to spawn.\n4. If timeout → hard kill:\n   a. tmux kill-session -t <session>.\n   b. Supervisor directly invokes .agent/hooks/_shared/kill-transcript-sync.sh and .agent/hooks/_shared/rotate-on-session-end.sh with the recorded session_id — recovers ~100% of what session-end.sh would have done because those shared scripts are already vendor-agnostic.\n   c. Log \"forced-kill fallback for session <name>\" to ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/respawn_log.log.\n5. Spawn new session: tmux new-session -d -s <new-name> '<vendor-cli-invocation>' — CLI as pane-1 process so clean exits auto-close the tmux session.\n6. Vendor's session-start hook fires on the fresh CLI and injects the generic seed prompt: \"Please familiarize yourself with this repo. Once you have done so, please set autonomous mode to DEEP.\" The seed prompt carries no state from the previous session — intent in the intent file is the only continuity.\n\n### Hook changes\n\n- Stop hook (all vendors): add one line to touch <session>.heartbeat. No other behavior change in the stop hook.\n- Circuit-breaker path in the stop hook: change loop-toggle off to loop-toggle --set-session-mode off (the new split form that only affects AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt, not autonomous_intent.txt).\n- Per-turn hooks (post-tool-use-transcript.sh for Claude Code / Codex / Cursor; after-agent.sh for Gemini): add one line to touch <session>.heartbeat. Session_id is already in hand.\n- Long-running-command wrappers (auto-pr, bakeoff-broad, bakeoff-deep, pytest smart-test): add a periodic heartbeat-touch inside each script's main loop. For pytest this is probably every test-file completion; for auto-pr it's every CI poll tick; for bakeoff it's every cohort or every run-step.\n- Session-start hook (all vendors): injects the generic seed prompt. Cross-vendor config documented in a new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section of AGENTS.md, including the vendor-specific exit keystroke used in replacement step 1.\n\n### The supervisor script\n\nscripts/agent-supervisor is a single long-running Python daemon with three subcommands:\n- run — enters the poll loop. Default cadence 60s. Configurable via flag.\n- status — one-shot print of current state (session inventory, heartbeat ages, autonomous_intent value).\n- stop — touches a sentinel that the running loop checks each iteration and exits cleanly.\n\nPer-workstation invariant: at most one supervisor per project, enforced by a pid-file + flock at ~/.cache/hypergumbo/agent-supervisor/<hashed_project>.lock. Supervisor only touches tmux sessions whose name matches a reserved prefix (hypergumbo-session-*) — anything else is ignored, so humans running their own tmux work are untouched. No cron, no systemd — the user launches scripts/agent-supervisor run once per workstation; persistence across reboots is their deployment choice (systemd --user unit, tmux detach, screen, whatever).\n\n### Safety rails\n\n- Sessions are not rhetorically linked (\"chain\" framing is dropped). Each new session starts fresh from the generic seed prompt with no inherited scratch notes.\n- Soft rate limit: sessions_started_today counter in a small JSON sentinel; exceeding N (proposal: 8) triggers a cooldown rather than another spawn. Throttling only; not a chain cap.\n- Append-only ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/respawn_log.log for retrospectives to quantify whether respawns actually produce work.\n- If the supervisor cannot spawn (tmux missing, vendor CLI missing), log and cooldown; do not retry tightly.\n\n## Roads not taken (brief)\n\n- Having the stop hook or a dying CLI spawn its own successor: rejected — the exiting session should not have a veto over its replacement, and that's what the 1M-token-era rationalization pattern exploits.\n- Cron/systemd-driven supervisor: rejected — a single long-running Python script is simpler and lets the user choose their own persistence mechanism.\n- Supervisor reading agent_notes.json as a decision input: rejected — narrative-shaped files cannot be supervisor-trustable even if they happen to be accurate today.\n- Using heartbeat as the load-bearing \"is this session alive?\" signal: rejected — it can't distinguish \"long bakeoff with no PostToolUse events\" from \"agent exited,\" and pane-delta is strictly stronger.\n- Chain-length caps and parent-pointers in meta.json: rejected along with the chain framing.\n\n## Governance\n\nTouches .agent/hooks/**, adds scripts/agent-supervisor, adds scripts/loop-toggle flags, documents a new AGENTS.md section. All governance-gated — stays needs_human_review until every implementation item is approved.\n\n## Open items to decompose (stays needs_human_review)\n\n1. scripts/agent-supervisor daemon — Python script, state-file format (meta.json / heartbeat / stopped sentinel), decision matrix as above, run/status/stop subcommands, flock lock, reserved-prefix discipline, respawn log, rate limit. No hook changes yet.\n\n2. Intent/mode split — scripts/loop-toggle grows two modes: --set-intent (writes autonomous_intent.txt only) and --set-session-mode (writes AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt only). The existing bareword form updates both (preserving today's UX). Circuit-breaker path in stop_logic.sh switches to --set-session-mode off. New autonomous_intent.txt file with initial value.\n\n3. Heartbeat instrumentation — per-vendor PostToolUse-equivalent hook adds heartbeat-touch; stop hook adds heartbeat-touch; long-running-command wrappers (auto-pr, bakeoff-*, pytest) add periodic heartbeat-touch. Telemetry only; not in the supervisor's decision path.\n\n4. Replacement sequence — tmux send-keys with vendor-specific exit keystroke, 30s graceful-exit poll, hard-kill fallback that calls kill-transcript-sync.sh + rotate-on-session-end.sh directly. Audit those shared scripts for idempotency when per-session files are already absent.\n\n5. Vendor parity documentation — new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section in AGENTS.md listing, per vendor: PostToolUse-equivalent hook name, session-start hook file path, graceful-exit keystroke sequence, CLI invocation string for tmux new-session.\n\n6. stop_hook_state.json write discipline — add an explicit del() clause to the jq merge in stop_logic.sh for any key not in the maintained set, so future migrations or manual edits cannot silently reintroduce zombies. Document the exact maintained-field list in the recover-state playbook (currently done implicitly).\n\nDecomposition happens after human confirmation that this design matches intent."  # 712e
