  message: "Final consolidated design after 2026-04-17..18 discussion. Replaces all prior thread entries.\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe stop-hook circuit breaker permits termination when the agent is stuck, which gives a stagnating agent a shortcut out of a DEEP session with ready backlog. Rewording AGENTS.md has repeatedly failed (WI-vapap, WI-furap, WI-duput — all closed) because the agent already had the rules and ignored them. In the 1M-token era, contexts rarely get compacted, so agents almost always exit by deliberately tripping the circuit breaker. Inside a running CLI there is no way to trigger a context clear, so the structural fix is to spawn a fresh CLI session with a clean context when the current one stops being productive.\n\n## Evidence gathered 2026-04-18\n\n- Circuit-breaker trip volume has accelerated sharply: ~12/day baseline across March, then 49 on 04/15, 80 on 04/16, 74 on 04/17. 6-8x acceleration consistent with \"agent is running out the clock.\"\n- Narrative content in agent_notes.json, sampled across 04/11 → 04/17 cooldown files (the cooldown archive bakes LAST REFLECTION NOTES into each file), does NOT show visible narrative rot — concrete PR numbers, tracker IDs, and explicit next-action statements throughout.\n- stop_hook_state.json had five zombie fields (last_pr, last_pr_num, last_pr_state, pending_hard_todos, pending_soft_todos) with no writer anywhere in the current codebase: a migration in PR #2926 (commit 670a07fdf) copied them into the new file, the reviewer-driven cleanup (commit 190206c76) deleted the migration without replacement writers, and the additive jq merge pattern in stop_logic.sh preserved the migrated values indefinitely. Full-repo grep confirmed no code reads them. Cleanup performed 2026-04-18: the keys were deleted from the live file via jq del().\n\nUpshot: the real failure mode is hook-side write gaps plus circuit-breaker volume, not agent narrative dishonesty. The supervisor should rely on event-shaped signals that are true by construction, not on fields an agent or a forgotten migration might have written.\n\n## Design: what we will build\n\n### Signal layer (what the supervisor reads)\n\nSplit \"project intent\" from \"current-session mode\":\n\n- autonomous_intent.txt — new file, lives at repo root next to AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt, gitignored. The human's desired mode for this project. Values: DEEP | BROAD | OFF. Only changed by explicit human invocation of loop-toggle (which gets a new --set-intent form). Never touched by the circuit breaker.\n- AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt — current running session's mode, same semantics as today, still written by loop-toggle and the circuit-breaker path. Not a supervisor input.\n- ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/agent-supervisor/<session>.meta.json — records session_id, CLI PID, vendor, project_dir, tmux session name, start UTC. Written once on spawn. Per-workstation state, consistent with where stop_hook_state.json / agent_notes.json / guidance_log already live.\n- ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/agent-supervisor/<session>.heartbeat — file mtime is touched by each vendor's per-turn hook (PostToolUse for Claude Code / Codex / Cursor; after-agent-response for Gemini CLI) plus by long-running-command wrappers (auto-pr, bakeoff-*, pytest smart-test). Telemetry, not a supervisor decision input — useful for debugging staleness and producing metrics but never load-bearing for spawn/no-spawn.\n- kill -0 <recorded_CLI_PID> — vendor-agnostic liveness probe.\n- tmux capture-pane -t <session> -p | wc -c — byte count of the pane's scrollback. Its delta-over-time is the primary \"is this session actually doing work?\" signal. Catches long-running tools that print progress output, catches truly-unanticipated activity, robust to hook gaps.\n- tmux list-clients -t <session> — any attached client blocks replacement (human is present).\n\n### Decision matrix\n\nOn each poll (default 60s cadence):\n\n| autonomous_intent | clients attached | PID alive | pane changed in last 15m | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OFF | — | — | — | do nothing (human quietness) |\n| non-OFF | yes | — | — | do nothing (human present) |\n| non-OFF | no | yes | yes | do nothing (working) |\n| non-OFF | no | yes | no | replace (idle — post-circuit-break, hung, or clean-exit waiting for input) |\n| non-OFF | no | no | — | replace (crashed or exited) |\n| non-OFF (no tmux session for this slot) | — | — | — | spawn fresh |\n\nThe supervisor does not need to distinguish clean-exit from crash from hung — all three route to the same \"replace\" path. The circuit-breaker trip (CLI alive but idle at prompt, pane frozen since the breaker's print) lands in the \"replace\" row.\n\n### Replacement sequence\n\nWhen the supervisor decides to replace a session:\n\n1. tmux send-keys -t <session>:0 \"<vendor-exit-keystroke>\" Enter — inject the vendor's graceful-exit command into the idle CLI's input.\n2. Poll kill -0 <CLI_PID> until the process is gone, 30s timeout.\n3. If CLI exited cleanly within timeout → the session-end hook fired → transcripts rotated via rotate-on-session-end.sh → transcript-sync watcher was killed via kill-transcript-sync.sh. Nothing else to do; tmux session auto-closes because the CLI was the pane's sole process. Proceed to spawn.\n4. If timeout → hard kill:\n   a. tmux kill-session -t <session>.\n   b. Supervisor directly invokes .agent/hooks/_shared/kill-transcript-sync.sh and .agent/hooks/_shared/rotate-on-session-end.sh with the recorded session_id — recovers ~100% of what session-end.sh would have done because those shared scripts are already vendor-agnostic.\n   c. Log \"forced-kill fallback for session <name>\" to ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/agent-supervisor/respawn_log.log.\n5. Spawn new session with the respawn-marker env var set:\n   tmux new-session -d -s <new-name> -e HYPERGUMBO_RESPAWN=1 '<vendor-cli-invocation>'\n   CLI as the pane-1 process so clean exits auto-close the tmux session.\n6. Vendor's session-start hook fires on the fresh CLI and checks $HYPERGUMBO_RESPAWN:\n   - Set (supervisor continuation) → auto-enable autonomous mode per autonomous_intent.txt AND inject the generic seed prompt so the agent begins working immediately: \"Please familiarize yourself with this repo. Once you have done so, please set autonomous mode to DEEP.\"\n   - Unset (human launched the CLI directly) → existing behavior: prompt the human to confirm OFF / DEEP / BROAD.\n\n### Hook changes\n\n- Stop hook (all vendors): add one line to touch <session>.heartbeat. No other behavior change.\n- Circuit-breaker path in stop_logic.sh: change loop-toggle off to loop-toggle --set-session-mode off (the new split form that only affects AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt, not autonomous_intent.txt).\n- Per-turn hooks (post-tool-use-transcript.sh for Claude Code / Codex / Cursor; after-agent.sh for Gemini): add one line to touch <session>.heartbeat. Session_id already in hand.\n- Long-running-command wrappers (auto-pr, bakeoff-broad, bakeoff-deep, pytest smart-test): add a periodic heartbeat-touch inside each script's main loop — every CI poll tick for auto-pr, every cohort/run-step for bakeoff, every test-file completion for pytest.\n- Session-start hook (all vendors): branch on $HYPERGUMBO_RESPAWN as described in step 6 above. Cross-vendor config documented in a new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section of AGENTS.md, including the vendor-specific exit keystroke used in replacement step 1.\n\n### The supervisor script\n\nscripts/agent-supervisor is a single long-running Python daemon with three subcommands:\n- run — enters the poll loop. Default cadence 60s. Configurable via flag.\n- status — one-shot print of current state (session inventory, heartbeat ages, autonomous_intent value).\n- stop — touches a sentinel that the running loop checks each iteration and exits cleanly.\n\nPer-workstation invariant: at most one supervisor per project, enforced by pid-file + flock at ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/agent-supervisor/supervisor.lock. Supervisor only touches tmux sessions whose name matches a reserved prefix (hypergumbo-session-*) — anything else is ignored, so humans running their own tmux work are untouched. No cron, no systemd — the user launches scripts/agent-supervisor run once per workstation; persistence across reboots is their deployment choice.\n\n### Concrete user UX\n\nFirst-time setup on a workstation:\n  ./scripts/loop-toggle DEEP          # writes autonomous_intent=DEEP + session_mode=DEEP\n  ./scripts/agent-supervisor run &    # starts the daemon in background\n\nFrom then on, the supervisor owns the tmux-session lifecycle.\n\nNormal operation:\n- Supervisor spawns a hypergumbo-session-<UTC> tmux session as needed.\n- To watch: tmux attach -t hypergumbo-session-<UTC>. Session name visible via ./scripts/agent-supervisor status. Detach with Ctrl-B D. Any attached client blocks replacement (by design), so watching is safe.\n- To pause the loop: ./scripts/loop-toggle OFF flips intent to OFF. Supervisor stops spawning; any live session finishes its current work and goes idle.\n- To resume: ./scripts/loop-toggle DEEP (or BROAD). Supervisor picks up on the next poll tick (≤60s).\n- To shut down for the day: ./scripts/agent-supervisor stop touches a sentinel; the daemon exits cleanly on its next poll.\n\nEdge cases:\n- Two supervisors for the same project: second one's flock acquisition fails and it exits with a clear message.\n- User wants to run a CLI by hand without supervisor interference: either loop-toggle OFF first, or launch the CLI in a tmux session whose name does NOT start with hypergumbo-session- (supervisor ignores non-prefixed sessions entirely).\n- Supervisor crashes: nothing gets auto-spawned until the user restarts it with ./scripts/agent-supervisor run &.\n\n### Safety rails\n\n- Each new session starts fresh from the generic seed prompt with no explicitly injected scratch notes.\n- Soft rate limit: sessions_started_today counter in a small JSON sentinel; exceeding N (proposal: 8) triggers a cooldown rather than another spawn. Throttling only; not a cap.\n- Append-only ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/agent-supervisor/respawn_log.log for retrospectives to quantify whether respawns actually produce work.\n- If the supervisor cannot spawn (tmux missing, vendor CLI missing), log and cooldown; do not retry tightly.\n\n## Roads not taken (brief)\n\n- Having the stop hook or a dying CLI spawn its own successor — the exiting session should not have a veto over its replacement.\n- Cron/systemd-driven supervisor — a single long-running Python script is simpler.\n- Supervisor reading agent_notes.json as a decision input — narrative-shaped files cannot be supervisor-trustable.\n- Using heartbeat as the load-bearing \"is this session alive?\" signal — pane-delta is strictly stronger for long-running-tool cases.\n- Chain-length caps and parent-pointers in meta.json — the chain framing was dropped.\n- Storing supervisor state under ~/.cache/ — inconsistent with existing ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/ convention.\n\n## Governance\n\nTouches .agent/hooks/**, adds scripts/agent-supervisor, adds scripts/loop-toggle flags, documents a new AGENTS.md section. All governance-gated — stays needs_human_review until every implementation item is approved.\n\n## Open items to decompose (stays needs_human_review)\n\n1. scripts/agent-supervisor daemon — Python script, state-file format (meta.json / heartbeat / stopped sentinel) under ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/agent-supervisor/, decision matrix as above, run/status/stop subcommands, flock lock, reserved-prefix discipline, respawn log, rate limit. No hook changes yet.\n\n2. Intent/mode split — scripts/loop-toggle grows two modes: --set-intent (writes autonomous_intent.txt only) and --set-session-mode (writes AUTONOMOUS_MODE.txt only). The existing bareword form updates both (preserving today's UX). Circuit-breaker path in stop_logic.sh switches to --set-session-mode off. New autonomous_intent.txt file at repo root, gitignored, with initial value.\n\n3. Heartbeat instrumentation — per-vendor PostToolUse-equivalent hook adds heartbeat-touch; stop hook adds heartbeat-touch; long-running-command wrappers (auto-pr, bakeoff-*, pytest) add periodic heartbeat-touch. Telemetry only; not in supervisor's decision path.\n\n4. Replacement sequence — tmux send-keys with vendor-specific exit keystroke, 30s graceful-exit poll, hard-kill fallback that calls kill-transcript-sync.sh + rotate-on-session-end.sh directly. Audit those shared scripts for idempotency when per-session files are already absent.\n\n5. Respawn-aware session-start hook — per-vendor branch on $HYPERGUMBO_RESPAWN: set → auto-enable autonomous mode from autonomous_intent.txt and inject the generic seed prompt; unset → existing OFF/DEEP/BROAD prompt.\n\n6. Vendor parity documentation — new \"Vendor Parity for Respawn\" section in AGENTS.md listing, per vendor: PostToolUse-equivalent hook name, session-start hook file path, graceful-exit keystroke sequence, CLI invocation string for tmux new-session.\n\n7. stop_hook_state.json write discipline — add an explicit del() clause to the jq merge in stop_logic.sh for any key not in the maintained set, so future migrations or manual edits cannot silently reintroduce zombies. Document the exact maintained-field list in the recover-state playbook.\n\nDecomposition happens after human confirmation that this design matches intent."  # 053a
